      "description": "A machine learning-based disease prediction system built as a final year AI & Data Science project. The system predicts the likelihood of a disease e.g., diabet...",
      "readme": "# 🩺 Disease Prediction System\n\nA machine learning-based disease prediction system built as a final year AI & Data Science project. The system predicts the likelihood of a disease (e.g., diabetes, heart disease) based on patient health parameters, helping in early risk detection.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 Project Overview\n\nEarly disease detection can significantly improve treatment outcomes. This project uses supervised machine learning to analyze patient health data (like glucose level, blood pressure, BMI, age, etc.) and predicts whether a patient is likely to have a particular disease.\n\nTwo common variants you can build (pick one, or both as separate modules):\n- **Diabetes Prediction** — using the Pima Indians Diabetes dataset.\n- **Heart Disease Prediction** — using the UCI Heart Disease dataset.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Objectives\n\n- Build a classification model that predicts disease risk from patient health metrics.\n- Perform data cleaning, exploratory data analysis (EDA), and feature engineering on medical data.\n- Compare multiple ML algorithms and select the best-performing one.\n- Provide a simple interface where users can input health parameters and get an instant prediction.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Tech Stack\n\n| Component        | Technology                          |\n|-------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| Language          | Python 3.x                          |\n| Data Handling     | Pandas, NumPy                       |\n| ML Algorithms     | Scikit-learn (Logistic Regression, Random Forest, SVM, KNN) |\n| Visualization     | Matplotlib, Seaborn                 |\n| Interface         | Streamlit (web app)                 |\n| Dataset           | Kaggle / UCI ML Repository (Pima Indians Diabetes / Heart Disease Dataset) |\n\n---\n\n## 📂 Project Structure\n\n```\ndisease-prediction-system/\n│\n├── data/\n│   └── disease_dataset.csv       # Patient health records dataset\n│\n├── notebooks/\n│   └── data_exploration.ipynb    # EDA and model experimentation\n│\n├── src/\n│   ├── preprocessing.py          # Data cleaning & feature scaling\n│   ├── train_model.py            # Model training & evaluation\n│   └── predict.py                # Load model & make predictions\n│\n├── models/\n│   └── disease_model.pkl         # Saved trained model\n│\n├── app.py                        # Streamlit web app\n├── requirements.txt\n└── README.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ How It Works\n\n1. **Data Preprocessing**\n   - Handle missing/zero values (common in medical datasets, e.g., glucose = 0 is invalid).\n   - Normalize/standardize numerical features (age, BMI, blood pressure, glucose level).\n   - Split data into training and testing sets.\n\n2. **Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)**\n   - Visualize feature distributions and correlations (e.g., heatmap of health parameters vs disease outcome).\n   - Identify key risk factors influencing the disease.\n\n3. **Model Training**\n   - Train multiple classifiers: Logistic Regression, Random Forest, SVM, KNN.\n   - Evaluate using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and ROC-AUC (important for medical predictions since false negatives are costly).\n   - Select the best model and save it using `pickle` or `joblib`.\n\n4. **Prediction Interface**\n   - User enters health parameters (age, glucose, BP, BMI, etc.) via a simple form.\n   - The saved model predicts disease risk (Positive/Negative) along with a confidence score.\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 Installation & Setup\n\n1. **Clone the repository**\n   ```bash\n   git clone https://github.com/yourusername/disease-prediction-system.git\n   cd disease-prediction-system\n   ```\n\n2. **Create a virtual environment (optional but recommended)**\n   ```bash\n   python -m venv venv\n   source venv/bin/activate    # On Windows: venv\\Scripts\\activate\n   ```\n\n3. **Install dependencies**\n   ```bash\n   pip install -r requirements.txt\n   ```\n\n4. **Train the model (if not already trained)**\n   ```bash\n   python src/train_model.py\n   ```\n\n5. **Run the Streamlit app**\n   ```bash\n   streamlit run app.py\n   ```\n\n6. Open the local URL shown in the terminal (usually `http://localhost:8501`) to use the app.\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Sample requirements.txt\n\n```\npandas\nnumpy\nscikit-learn\nstreamlit\nmatplotlib\nseaborn\njoblib\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🖥️ Usage\n\n- Enter patient health parameters (e.g., age, glucose level, blood pressure, BMI, insulin level).\n- Click \"Predict\" to get the disease risk result (e.g., \"High Risk\" / \"Low Risk\") with a probability score.\n- View feature importance charts to understand which factors influenced the prediction most.\n\n---\n\n## 📈 Results / Evaluation\n\n- Models compared using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and confusion matrix.\n- Random Forest / SVM typically perform best on structured medical datasets like Pima Diabetes or UCI Heart Disease.\n- Target: **~75–85% accuracy** is realistic and acceptable for these datasets at a final-year project level.\n\n*(Add your actual results, confusion matrix, and screenshots here once you run the model.)*\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Disclaimer\n\nThis project is built for **academic and educational purposes only**. It is not a certified medical diagnostic tool and should not be used for real clinical decision-making.\n\n---\n\n## 🔮 Future Enhancements\n\n- Extend to multi-disease prediction (diabetes, heart disease, kidney disease) in one app.\n- Use deep learning (ANN) for improved accuracy on larger datasets.\n- Add explainability using SHAP/LIME to show why a prediction was made (important for medical trust).\n- Deploy the app on Streamlit Cloud / Heroku for public access.\n- Add user authentication and prediction history tracking.\n\n---\n\n## 👥 Team\n\n**[Your Name]** — Data Preprocessing, Model Training\n**[Teammate Name]** — Streamlit App, Evaluation & Visualization\nFinal Year — AI & Data Science\n[Your College Name]\n\n---\n\n## 🤝 Contributing (Team Workflow)\n\n1. Clone the repo and create your own feature branch: `git checkout -b feature/your-task`\n2. Commit changes regularly with clear messages.\n3. Push your branch and open a Pull Request into `main`.\n4. Get review/approval from your teammate before merging.\n5. Pull the latest `main` before starting new work to stay in sync.\n\n---\n\n## 📄 License\n\nThis project is developed for academic purposes as part of a final year college project.\n",
